  "text": "התחיל\nTo solve this problem, we need to find the points of intersection between the parabola \\(x = 34y^2\\) and the piecewise linear function \\(f(x)\\). The piecewise function \\(f(x)\\) is defined as:\n\n\\[\nf(x) = \\begin{cases}\nx & \\text{if } x \\in [-1, 1) \\\\\n2 - x & \\text{if } x \\in [1, 3)\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nThe function is periodic with period 4, so we can restrict our attention to the interval \\([-1, 3)\\) and then extend the result to the entire real line.\n\n### Step-by-Step Solution:\n\n1. **Find the intersections in the interval \\([-1, 3)\\):**\n\n   We need to solve \\(34y^2 = x\\) for both pieces of the function.\n\n   - For \\(x \\in [-1, 1)\\):\n     \\[\n     34y^2 = x \\implies y = \\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{x}{34}}\n     \\]\n     Since \\(x \\in [-1, 1)\\), \\(y\\) must be in \\([-1/\\sqrt{34}, 1/\\sqrt{34})\\).\n\n   - For \\(x \\in [1, 3)\\):\n     \\[\n     34y^2 = 2 - x \\implies y = \\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2 - x}{34}}\n     \\]\n     Since \\(x \\in [1, 3)\\), \\(y\\) must be in \\([-1/\\sqrt{34}, 1/\\sqrt{34})\\).\n\n2. **Sum the \\(y\\)-coordinates:**\n\n   We need to sum the \\(y\\)-coordinates of all intersection points. Since the function is periodic with period 4, we can consider the intersections in one period and then multiply by the number of periods.\n\n   The sum of the \\(y\\)-coordinates in one period (from \\(-1\\) to \\(3\\)) is:\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{y \\in [-1/\\sqrt{34}, 1/\\sqrt{34})} \\left( \\sqrt{\\frac{x}{34}} + \\sqrt{\\frac{2 - x}{34}} \\right)\n   \\]\n   This sum can be simplified using the properties of the function and the periodicity.\n\n   The sum of the \\(y\\)-coordinates in one period is:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{34}} \\left( \\sum_{y \\in [-1/\\sqrt{34}, 1/\\sqrt{34})} \\left( \\sqrt{\\frac{x}{34}} + \\sqrt{\\frac{2 - x}{34}} \\right) \\right)\n   \\]\n   This sum can be evaluated numerically or analytically, but for simplicity, we can use the fact that the sum of the \\(y\\)-coordinates in one period is \\(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{34}} \\cdot 2 = \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{34}} = \\frac{2\\sqrt{34}}{34} = \\frac{\\sqrt{34}}{17}\\).\n\n3. **Sum the \\(y\\)-coordinates for all periods:**\n\n   Since the function is periodic with period 4, we need to sum the \\(y\\)-coordinates for 4 periods:\n   \\[\n   4 \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{34}}{17} = \\frac{4\\sqrt{34}}{17}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Express the sum in the form \\(\\frac{a + b\\sqrt{c}}{d}\\):**\n\n   The sum \\(\\frac{4\\sqrt{34}}{17}\\) is already in the desired form, where \\(a = 0\\), \\(b = 4\\), \\(c = 34\\), and \\(d = 17\\). Since \\(a, b,\\) and \\(d\\) have a greatest common divisor of 1 and \\(c\\) is not divisible by the square of any prime, we have:\n   \\[\n   a + b + c + d = 0 + 4 + 34 + 17 = 55\n   \\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{55}\n\\]",
